  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}-4(-1)^{3}+1=2+4+1=7 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{4}-4(0)^{3}+1=1 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)^{4}-4(1)^{3}+1=2-4+1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/fa4a3f723bb55521ec02dda8d088427b1939721366155f5ac45dd8e1429992e7.png)",
